WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.633
Gradients.

2
00:00:01.933 --> 00:00:05.177
Stack the slopes into one arrow, then walk the other way.

3
00:00:05.177 --> 00:00:06.600
That is how models learn.

4
00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:11.300
The gradient stacks all the partial derivatives into one vector.

5
00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:17.333
That vector points in the direction where the function climbs fastest, and its length tells you how steep it is.

6
00:00:17.633 --> 00:00:21.133
Which means the opposite direction is the fastest way down.

7
00:00:22.333 --> 00:00:24.000
Here is the whole update rule.

8
00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:27.100
Compute the gradient of the loss, which points uphill.

9
00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:29.200
Scale it by the learning rate, eta.

10
00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:31.220
Subtract it from the weights.

11
00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:35.133
That is gradient descent, repeated until the loss stops improving.

12
00:00:36.333 --> 00:00:37.900
Watch it on a simple bowl.

13
00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:41.267
The ball starts high on the curve and steps against the slope.

14
00:00:41.567 --> 00:00:45.233
As it nears the bottom the slope flattens, so the steps get smaller.

15
00:00:46.433 --> 00:00:47.833
The learning rate needs care.

16
00:00:48.133 --> 00:00:50.367
Too small, and you crawl toward the minimum.

17
00:00:50.667 --> 00:00:54.467
Too large, and you overshoot the valley wall, so the loss can blow up.

18
00:00:54.767 --> 00:00:57.567
That is why it is the classic first hyperparameter.

19
00:00:58.767 --> 00:01:00.133
Here it is in NumPy.

20
00:01:00.433 --> 00:01:02.833
We start at three, four, high on the bowl.

21
00:01:03.133 --> 00:01:07.667
The gradient of x squared plus y squared is simply two times the position.

22
00:01:07.967 --> 00:01:11.953
We step against it, twenty five times, and land near zero, zero.

23
00:01:11.953 --> 00:01:12.700
The minimum.

24
00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:17.067
Every loss curve in TensorBoard is a trace of this walk.

25
00:01:17.367 --> 00:01:20.733
If the loss explodes to NaN, the learning rate was too high.

26
00:01:21.033 --> 00:01:24.867
If it plateaus early, it may be too low, or you are on a flat region.

27
00:01:25.167 --> 00:01:30.233
And schedulers like warmup and cosine decay just modulate eta along the way.

28
00:01:31.433 --> 00:01:32.172
Let's recap.

29
00:01:32.172 --> 00:01:33.833
The gradient points uphill.

30
00:01:34.133 --> 00:01:35.867
Training steps the opposite way.

31
00:01:36.167 --> 00:01:38.133
The learning rate sets the step size.

32
00:01:38.433 --> 00:01:41.299
And every loss curve is a trace of this walk.

33
00:01:41.299 --> 00:01:42.700
Next up: the Jacobian.
